\title{APIGUARD: A Machine Learning-Assisted Framework for\\
Continuous API Security Assessment in DevSecOps Pipelines\\
\large Deliverables 2 and 3 --- System Model and Proposed Methodology}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Thornkasidej Fuangfoo (6622771234) \quad Varit Mesri (6622771317)}
\IEEEauthorblockA{Sirindhorn International Institute of Technology (SIIT),
Thammasat University, Pathum Thani, Thailand\\
Adviser: Somchart Fugkeaw}
\thanks{This deliverable describes the system for the registered topic
``Continuous API Security Assessment in DevSecOps Pipelines.'' It replaces the
Infrastructure-as-Code system presented in Deliverable~1, which was withdrawn because that
topic had already been reserved by another group. The target artefact is now the OpenAPI specification instead of Terraform code.}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Rule-based linting is the only security check most OpenAPI specifications receive before
deployment. A linter fires when a written rule matches, which leaves it unable to say which
findings matter, unable to distinguish an endpoint returning a health-check string from one
returning customer records, and silent about anything no rule covers. In a CI/CD pipeline
this produces alert fatigue and blind spots at the same time. This document specifies the
system model of APIGUARD, a framework that places a supervised classifier \emph{after} the
rule scanner rather than in place of it. The scanner keeps its authority over what rules
cover; the model assigns every endpoint a risk score, ranks the findings, and explains each
score with SHAP attributions, so that the pipeline gate acts on a risk score rather than on
an alert count. We define the entities and their trust relationships, the notation and the
gate decision rule, every runtime and offline component with its inputs, outputs and
guarantees, the data flow of a single pipeline run, the threat model and its stated limits,
and the deployment form. We also map each research question to the experiment that will
answer it. The methodology section then specifies how labels are made and kept apart from the
scanner's own output, the training algorithm, a gate that judges only new or changed endpoints,
and the rule hold-out protocol that tests whether the model generalises beyond the rules.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
API security, OpenAPI, DevSecOps, CI/CD, static analysis, XGBoost, SHAP, OWASP API Security
Top 10, risk scoring.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

%==============================================================
\section{Introduction}
Modern software is assembled out of HTTP APIs, and the OpenAPI specification is the first
place where an API's security posture is written down: which endpoints require
authentication, which scheme they use, what data comes back, and what limits apply. That
makes the specification a cheap and early place to catch security design mistakes, long
before a penetration test is run.

Today this checking is done by rules only. A linter such as Spectral with the OWASP ruleset
fires when a written rule matches. It cannot say which of its findings actually matter, it
produces the same alert whether an endpoint returns a health-check string or a customer's
date of birth, and it says nothing about a problem nobody wrote a rule for. Inside a CI/CD
pipeline this causes two failures at once: developers stop reading the alerts, and the gate
lets dangerous designs through.

Scale makes the opportunity concrete. The APIstic dataset contains 1{,}275{,}568 valid
OpenAPI specifications mined from GitHub, SwaggerHub, BigQuery and APIs.guru~\cite{apistic},
and the curated APIs.guru directory alone holds 2{,}529 API descriptions covering 108{,}837
endpoints~\cite{apisguru}. This is a large, machine-readable body of real-world API design,
and it is almost untouched as training data for security.

APIGUARD places a supervised model \emph{after} the rule scanner. The scanner keeps finding
what rules find; the model gives every endpoint a risk score, ranks the findings, and
explains each score with SHAP. The pipeline gate then acts on a risk score instead of a raw
alert count. This document specifies the system model: the entities involved, the notation
used, every component with its inputs and outputs, the data flow of a single pipeline run,
the trust assumptions, and the way the system is deployed. It then specifies the proposed
methodology and algorithms (Section~\ref{sec:method}).

The contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item A system model in which a classifier scores \emph{every} endpoint after the rule scanner,
so that the gate acts on a ranked, explained risk score instead of an alert count.
\item A label protocol that keeps scanner labels for training only and measures every result
against independently produced labels (L2 and L3), which removes the circularity of training and testing on scanner output.
\item A rule hold-out protocol (E2) that tests whether the model learned a design property
rather than a rule trigger.
\item A gate that judges only new or changed endpoints, so that existing APIs can adopt it.
\item A working implementation of the normaliser (S0), the feature builder (S2a, 66 features)
and the gate (S3), each with contract tests.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Changes since Deliverable 2.} The system model of Deliverable~2 is kept and revised
as follows: (i)~a fabricated author name was removed from the VAmPI reference; (ii)~the SHAP
contract now states that attributions sum to the raw margin, not to the score; (iii)~the claim
that a developer cannot change the threshold now names the mechanism that enforces it;
(iv)~the evaluation no longer scores the model against the scanner labels it trained on;
(v)~a language-model baseline and a text-inflation test were added; (vi)~the feature table now
matches the implemented feature builder; and (vii)~the gate counts only new or changed
endpoints.

%==============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Analysis of OpenAPI Specifications}
Serbout and Pautasso collected 1{,}275{,}568 OpenAPI specifications and published structural
metrics for them~\cite{apistic}; their work describes how APIs are written, not how safe the
designs are. Decrop \emph{et al.} automate the quality analysis of OpenAPI
specifications~\cite{oasquali}. The closest work to ours, Cheh and Chen, analyse OpenAPI
documents for security design issues with a graph model of sensitive-field exposure, but
evaluate it on a single API~\cite{cheh}. Tanveer \emph{et al.} survey the detection of
vulnerabilities in RESTful APIs~\cite{tanveer}.

\subsection{Rule-Based Scanning and Alert Prioritisation}
Spectral with the OWASP ruleset turns the API Security Top 10 into rules that fire on a
specification~\cite{spectralowasp,owasptop10}. A rule fires or it does not; it gives no ranking
and cannot report a problem nobody wrote a rule for. Flynn \emph{et al.} show that
classification models can prioritise the alerts of several static analysis tools~\cite{flynn}.
APIGUARD applies this idea at endpoint level to API specifications, and additionally scores
endpoints on which no rule fired.

\subsection{Weak Labels and Explanation}
Training on automatically produced labels is the weak-supervision setting of
Snorkel~\cite{snorkel}. Because such labels are noisy, we use them for training only.
Gradient-boosted trees~\cite{xgboost} are explained with SHAP~\cite{shap} and its exact
tree algorithm~\cite{treeshap}. Leakage between training and test data is a documented cause of
over-optimistic results~\cite{kapoor}, which is why every split here is grouped by provider.

\subsection{Gap}
To our knowledge, no existing work combines a learned, explained, endpoint-level risk score
with a pipeline gate and tests it on rules that were held out of training. This is the gap
APIGUARD addresses.

%==============================================================
\section{System Overview}
APIGUARD is organised into three lanes. Separating them is a design decision rather than a
drawing convenience: each lane runs at a different time, under different trust conditions,
and at a different speed.

\begin{itemize}
\item The \textbf{runtime lane} is what ships. It runs on every commit inside the CI/CD
pipeline, must finish in seconds, and is fully automatic.
\item The \textbf{offline lane} builds the model. It runs rarely, on a workstation or build
server, and may take hours.
\item The \textbf{feedback lane} is how a human verdict re-enters the system. It is
deliberately slow and never reaches the model directly.
\end{itemize}

Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} shows the three lanes and the two connections between them: the trained
model artefact loaded by the runtime, and the shared code path that guarantees training and
runtime compute features identically.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{architecture.pdf}
\caption{APIGUARD system model. The runtime lane (S0--S3) runs inside the pipeline; the
offline lane (T1--T4) produces \texttt{model.json}; the feedback lane returns human verdicts
to the label store. Amber marks shared code and the model artefact; dashed teal marks
the human-in-the-loop path. The unit of analysis is one endpoint.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure*}

%==============================================================
\section{Entities, Roles and Trust Assumptions}
Six entities take part in the system. Table~\ref{tab:entities} states the role of each and
how far it is trusted.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Entities, Their Roles and Trust Assumptions}
\label{tab:entities}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{L{2.4cm} Y Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Entity} & \textbf{Role in the system} & \textbf{Trust assumption}\\
\midrule
Developer & Writes or edits an OpenAPI specification and pushes it to the repository; reads
the gate output and fixes what it reports. & Not malicious, but assumed to make design
mistakes. Cannot change the gate threshold on a protected branch: the workflow file is guarded
by branch protection or an organisation-level reusable workflow.\\
CI/CD runner & Checks out the repository, invokes the APIGUARD CLI, and acts on its exit
code. & Trusted execution environment; its configuration is under version control.\\
APIGUARD CLI & The runtime lane, S0--S3. Reads a specification and produces a per-endpoint
risk report and a pass/fail exit code. & The component under study. Must be deterministic
for a fixed specification, ruleset version and model.\\
Rule scanner & Third-party linter (Spectral with the OWASP ruleset). Supplies findings used
as a baseline and as model input. & Trusted but incomplete. Version is pinned, because a
version change silently changes every label.\\
Security analyst (here, the two team members) & Reviews a sample of gate verdicts, marks each correct or incorrect, and
owns the retraining decision. & Authoritative for labels. The only entity allowed to change
what the model learns.\\
Offline trainer & Builds the corpus, assembles labels, trains the model and emits
\texttt{model.json}. & Runs outside the pipeline. Never invoked automatically by a gate
decision.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

%==============================================================
\section{Notation and Definitions}
Table~\ref{tab:notation} fixes the notation used here and in every later deliverable.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Notation Used Throughout the Project}
\label{tab:notation}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{L{2.6cm} Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Meaning}\\
\midrule
$S$ & An OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0 specification document; the input to one pipeline run.\\
$e=(p,m)$ & One \emph{endpoint}: a path $p$ combined with an HTTP method $m$. This is the
unit of analysis.\\
$E(S)$ & The set of all endpoints declared in $S$, produced by the normaliser S0.\\
$A(e)$ & The scanner findings attributed to $e$, each carrying a rule identifier, a severity
and an OWASP API Top 10 category.\\
$x(e)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ & The feature vector of $e$, produced by S2a from the normalised
specification and from $A(e)$.\\
$y(e)\in\{0,1\}$ & The label of $e$: 1 if the endpoint carries a security-relevant design
issue, 0 otherwise.\\
$f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\!\rightarrow\![0,1]$ & The trained classifier (XGBoost), loaded from
\texttt{model.json} at CLI startup.\\
$r(e)$ & The risk score of $e$, defined as $r(e)=f(x(e))$.\\
$\phi(e)$ & The SHAP attribution vector for $e$: the contribution of each feature to the raw margin
(log-odds) from which $r(e)$ is computed.\\
$\tau,\;k$ & The gate threshold and the tolerated count of endpoints at or above it.\\
$G(S)$ & The gate decision for the whole specification: $0$ = pass, $1$ = fail.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

For one specification $S$, the runtime lane computes the scored endpoint set
\begin{equation}
R(S)=\bigl\{\,\bigl(e,\;r(e),\;\phi(e)\bigr)\;:\;e\in E(S)\,\bigr\},
\label{eq:scored}
\end{equation}
and the gate decision is
\begin{equation}
G(S)=
\begin{cases}
1, & \bigl|\{\,e\in E(S)\;:\;r(e)\ge\tau\,\}\bigr| > k,\\[2pt]
0, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\label{eq:gate}
\end{equation}

With the default policy $k=0$ the gate fails if any single endpoint scores at or above
$\tau$. A larger $k$ lets a team adopt the tool gradually on an existing API without
blocking every build on the first day. The value of $\tau$ is not chosen by hand: it is
swept on a validation set and reported as a precision/recall trade-off curve
(Experiment~E3).

Two properties the design must hold. First, $r(e)$ is defined for \emph{every} endpoint,
including endpoints on which no rule fired; for those, the scanner-derived features are
simply zero. Second, for a fixed specification, ruleset version and model artefact the whole
runtime lane is deterministic --- nothing in it samples randomly.

%==============================================================
\section{Runtime Lane: Components S0--S3}

\subsection{S0 --- Normaliser}
\textbf{Input:} a specification file in YAML or JSON, either OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0.
\textbf{Output:} a list of normalised endpoint records, one per (path, method) pair.

The normaliser resolves internal \texttt{\$ref} pointers, expands the schema of every request
body and response, and flattens the two specification versions into one internal form, so
that no component after S0 needs to know which version the document came from. Path-level
parameters are merged into each operation, and a global \texttt{security} block is propagated
to operations that do not declare their own --- recording, importantly, whether the operation
\emph{inherited} or \emph{overrode} that requirement. An operation that explicitly overrides
global security with an empty requirement is a different risk from one that simply inherits
it, and a design that collapsed the two would lose that distinction before the model ever
saw it.

\textbf{Guarantees.} Every endpoint of a parsable specification appears exactly once in the
output. A specification that cannot be parsed is not silently skipped: it is written to an
error log with the reason, and the corpus statistics report the parse-failure rate. Circular
references are cut at a fixed depth so that expansion always terminates.

\subsection{S1 --- Rule Scanner}
\textbf{Input:} the original specification file. \textbf{Output:} the set $A(e)$ of findings
for each endpoint.

S1 runs Spectral with \texttt{@stoplight/\allowbreak spectral-\allowbreak owasp-\allowbreak ruleset}~v2.x, which implements the
OWASP API Security Top 10, 2023 edition~\cite{owasptop10,spectralowasp}. Spectral reports
findings against JSON paths inside the document; S1 maps each finding down to the endpoint it
belongs to, so that a document-level finding is attributed to every endpoint and an
operation-level finding only to its own. The 42Crunch audit score may optionally be collected
as an additional signal.

S1 output is used in three distinct ways, and keeping them apart matters for the evaluation:
it is the \textbf{baseline} the model is compared against, it is the source of the
\textbf{automatic labels} used for training, and it enters the model as \textbf{input
features}. The scanner and ruleset versions are pinned and recorded in every report, because
a ruleset upgrade changes both the baseline and the labels at the same time.

\subsection{S2 --- AI Verifier}
S2 is the core of the contribution and has three sub-components.

\textbf{S2a Feature builder.} Turns one normalised endpoint record, plus its scanner
findings, into a fixed-length numeric vector $x(e)$. The features fall into the six groups of
Table~\ref{tab:features}. The builder is a tested module with no dependency on the training
pipeline, which is what allows the same code to be imported by both S2a and T3.

\textbf{S2b Classifier.} An XGBoost gradient-boosted tree ensemble~\cite{xgboost}, loaded
from \texttt{model.json} at startup and applied to every endpoint. Gradient boosting is
chosen because the input is a table of mixed numeric, binary and categorical features,
because strong class imbalance is handled directly through \texttt{scale\_pos\_weight}, and
because tree ensembles remain traceable back to individual features. Simpler baselines ---
majority class, logistic regression and random forest --- are trained on the identical
feature table for comparison.

\textbf{S2c Explainer.} SHAP~\cite{shap} produces $\phi(e)$, attributing the score to
individual features using Shapley values from cooperative game theory, computed exactly for tree ensembles by TreeSHAP~\cite{treeshap}. Every finding APIGUARD
reports carries the three to five features that pushed its score up. This is not decoration:
it is how a developer checks whether the model reasoned about a security property or about
something irrelevant, and it is how the team detects a model that has learned a shortcut.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Feature Groups Computed by S2a From One Normalised Endpoint}
\label{tab:features}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{L{2.2cm} Y L{4.6cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Group} & \textbf{Contents} & \textbf{Why it should carry signal}\\
\midrule
A. Structural & Path depth; number of path parameters; whether a path parameter looks like an
object identifier (\texttt{id}, \texttt{uuid}, \texttt{\_id}); HTTP method; query and header
parameter counts; request body present; response schema property count; declared status
codes; whether 401/403 are declared; deprecated flag. & An object identifier in the path with
no ownership check is the classic API1 Broken Object Level Authorization shape.\\
B. Security scheme & Whether the endpoint has a security requirement; inherited versus
overridden; scheme type (apiKey, http-basic, http-bearer, oauth2, openIdConnect); scope
count; whether the server URL uses \texttt{http} rather than \texttt{https}. & The direct
expression of authentication and authorisation design (API2, API5).\\
C. Data sensitivity & Count of response property names matching a sensitive-term lexicon
(password, token, secret, ssn, dob, phone, email, address, card, balance); maximum term
weight; sensitive properties in response versus request; ratio of sensitive to total
properties; the combination flag \emph{no security requirement and sensitive response
fields}. & What a rule cannot see: it separates a health-check endpoint from one returning
customer data (API3).\\
D. Documentation hygiene & Description and summary lengths; example present; share of
parameters with a description or example; version segment in the path; pagination parameters present; rate-limit headers
documented. & Poorly specified endpoints correlate with weak design; rate-limit documentation
relates to API4.\\
E. Scanner-derived & \texttt{flagged\_by\_spectral}, \texttt{rule\_count},
\texttt{severity\_max}, \texttt{owasp\_categories\_hit}, optionally
\texttt{42crunch\_audit\_score}. All zero when nothing fired. & Lets the model use the rules
without depending on them. Kept separable so the ablation can remove the group entirely.\\
F. Context & Endpoint count of the parent specification; whether the file is OpenAPI 3.x
or Swagger 2.0. Provider identity is deliberately excluded and used only to group the splits. & Places the endpoint inside its API rather than judging it in
isolation.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

\subsection{S3 --- Risk Gate}
\textbf{Input:} $R(S)$, the scored endpoint set. \textbf{Output:} a report and a process exit
code.

S3 applies the decision rule of~\eqref{eq:gate} and emits the ranked findings in one of three
formats: human-readable text for a terminal, JSON for further processing, and SARIF so that
results appear as annotations in the code-review interface. The exit code is 0 on pass and 1
on fail, which is the only part of the system the CI runner needs to understand. The report
always lists endpoints in descending risk order, and each entry carries the score, the SHAP
reasons, and any scanner findings that fired.

%==============================================================
\section{Offline Lane: Components T1--T4}

\subsection{T1 --- Corpus}
Table~\ref{tab:corpora} lists the four sources. Each downloaded specification is stored with
its source URL and a snapshot date, and is de-duplicated by normalised content hash before
anything else happens. Public API specifications are heavily copied and templated between
projects; near-duplicates left in the corpus would appear on both sides of a train/test
split.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Corpora and Their Role in the Project}
\label{tab:corpora}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\columnwidth}{L{1.7cm} L{1.9cm} Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Source} & \textbf{Size} & \textbf{Use}\\
\midrule
APIs.guru~\cite{apisguru} & 2{,}529 APIs, 108{,}837 endpoints & Primary corpus; curated,
clean, version-stable.\\
APIstic~\cite{apistic} & 1{,}275{,}568 specifications & Scale-up if the schedule allows;
reference for structural metrics.\\
VAmPI~\cite{vampi} & 1 API, vulnerable \emph{and} secure mode & Ground truth; the same API in
two configurations gives matched positive and negative examples.\\
OWASP crAPI~\cite{crapi} & 1 API, documented challenges & Second ground-truth target,
independent of VAmPI.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\subsection{T2 --- Label Store}
Three label sources are kept separate and never merged into one undifferentiated pile.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{L1 automatic.} Spectral OWASP findings mapped down to endpoints. Cheap and
plentiful, and circular if used alone --- a model trained only on L1 can at best reproduce
the scanner.
\item \textbf{L2 reference labels.} A stratified sample of 304 endpoints labelled independently
by three language-model passes that never saw scanner output or model scores, combined by
majority vote, with agreement reported as Cohen's and Fleiss' $\kappa$. No human labelled
these endpoints. Small and imperfect, but independent of the scanner, which is what the
false-positive claim needs.
\item \textbf{L3 ground truth.} VAmPI and crAPI endpoints with known, documented
vulnerabilities.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{T3 --- Shared Core}
T3 is not a separate implementation. It is the S0 normaliser and the S2a feature builder
\emph{imported} by the training pipeline, not copied into it. This is a deliberate
constraint: if training and runtime computed features from two code paths, any drift between
them would appear as an unexplained drop in live accuracy that no experiment could diagnose.
The amber path in Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} marks this shared code.

\subsection{T4 --- Trainer}
T4 takes the labelled feature table and produces \texttt{model.json}. Four rules govern it.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Split by provider or repository, never at random.} A random split leaks
near-identical endpoints across the boundary and produces impressive but meaningless scores.
Grouped 5-fold cross-validation is used throughout.
\item \textbf{Report PR-AUC, F1 and MCC, not accuracy.} Insecure endpoints are a small
minority; a model that predicted ``secure'' for everything would score high on accuracy
alone.
\item \textbf{Fix every random seed and pin every dependency version.} The scanner version,
ruleset version, corpus snapshot date and seed are written into the model artefact's
metadata.
\item \textbf{Emit an artefact, not a live service.} \texttt{model.json} is a file under
version control, so any reported result can be reproduced with the exact model that produced
it.
\end{enumerate}

%==============================================================
\section{Feedback Lane}
When a developer or analyst disagrees with a verdict, the decision is appended to
\texttt{feedback.jsonl} as one record per endpoint: the endpoint identity, the specification
hash, the score it received, the human verdict, the reviewer and a timestamp. The queue is an
input to T2 at the \emph{next deliberate retrain}, run by a person who has looked at what is
in the queue.

There is deliberately \textbf{no arrow from S3 back into S2b}. A model that consumes its own
accepted predictions as training data reinforces its own mistakes and drifts away from the
ground truth, with the drift hidden by the model's own agreement with itself. Human verdicts
are therefore treated as new labels, not as confirmation.

%==============================================================
\section{End-to-End Data Flow}
One pipeline run, in order:

\begin{enumerate}
\item A developer pushes a commit that touches \texttt{openapi.yaml}.
\item The CI runner checks out the repository and invokes
\texttt{apiguard scan openapi.yaml}\linebreak[1] \texttt{-{}-threshold 0.70}.
\item \textbf{S0} parses the file, resolves references and emits $E(S)$ --- say 48 endpoint
records.
\item \textbf{S1} runs Spectral with the pinned OWASP ruleset and attributes each finding to
an endpoint, giving $A(e)$. Suppose 11 endpoints are flagged.
\item \textbf{S2a} builds $x(e)$ for all 48 endpoints, not only the 11. For the other 37,
group~E features are zero.
\item \textbf{S2b} loads \texttt{model.json} once and scores all 48, producing $r(e)$.
\item \textbf{S2c} computes $\phi(e)$ and keeps the top contributing features per endpoint.
\item \textbf{S3} ranks by $r(e)$, applies $\tau$ and $k$, writes the report and exits 0 or
1. An unflagged endpoint may appear at the top of the ranking --- for example
\texttt{GET /users/\{id\}} with no security requirement and a response containing
\texttt{email} and \texttt{phone}.
\item The runner fails the build on exit code~1 and surfaces the SARIF annotations in the
pull request.
\item The developer either fixes the specification or marks the finding as a false positive,
which appends a record to \texttt{feedback.jsonl}.
\item At the next retrain, an analyst reviews the queue, T2 updates the labels, T4 produces a
new \texttt{model.json}, and the new artefact is committed.
\end{enumerate}

%==============================================================
\section{Interfaces and Data Contracts}
Table~\ref{tab:contracts} states what each component accepts, what it returns, and the
contract it must satisfy. These contracts are what allow the components to be unit-tested in
isolation.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\caption{Component Interfaces and Their Contracts}
\label{tab:contracts}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{L{1.5cm} L{4.0cm} L{4.0cm} Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Output} & \textbf{Contract}\\
\midrule
S0 & Specification file (YAML/JSON) & Normalised endpoint records & Every endpoint appears
once; parse failures are logged, never dropped silently.\\
S1 & Specification file & Findings attributed per endpoint & The ruleset version is recorded
in the output.\\
S2a & Endpoint record and its findings & Fixed-length feature vector & Same length and
ordering for every endpoint; no null values.\\
S2b & Feature vector & Score in $[0,1]$ & Deterministic for a fixed model artefact.\\
S2c & Feature vector and model & SHAP attributions & Attributions sum to the raw margin (log-odds) minus the base margin;
$r(e)$ is the logistic transform of that margin.\\
S3 & Scored endpoint set & Report and exit code & Exit code is 0 or 1 only; the report is
always ranked by score.\\
T4 & Labelled feature table & \texttt{model.json} & Carries seed, tool versions and corpus
snapshot date in its metadata.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

%==============================================================
\section{Assumptions and Threat Model}
\textbf{What is trusted.} The CI runner and the repository are trusted. The specification file
is trusted as an \emph{input}, not as a \emph{claim}: APIGUARD reads what it says but does not
assume it is true of the running service.

\textbf{Who the adversary is.} The adversary is an external attacker interacting with the
deployed API through its public interface, looking for endpoints that expose data without
adequate authentication or authorisation, that leak sensitive fields, or that lack rate
limiting. APIGUARD does not model an insider who tampers with the pipeline configuration, and
it is not a defence against a developer who deliberately weakens the specification and lowers
$\tau$ at the same time. The model can also be gamed: group~D features can be inflated by
writing longer prose, so Experiment~E5 includes a text-inflation test (Section~\ref{sec:method}).

\textbf{Assumptions.} (i)~The specification is a reasonably faithful description of the
implemented API; where it is not, APIGUARD's finding is still useful as a documentation
defect. (ii)~Endpoints can be judged largely independently of one another; cross-endpoint
attack chains are out of scope. (iii)~The corpus of public specifications is representative
enough of real-world API design to train on --- an assumption tested directly by
Experiment~E4.

\textbf{The central limitation, stated honestly.} A specification can be secure on paper while
the implementation behind it is not. APIGUARD measures \emph{design-level} risk. It is not a
substitute for dynamic testing and is not claimed to be. Experiment~E4 measures how far
design-level scoring carries into two deliberately vulnerable applications, and that
measurement --- whatever it shows --- is reported as part of the result.

\textbf{Out of scope.} Source-code analysis of API implementations; GraphQL, gRPC and SOAP;
live traffic analysis and WAF-style blocking; and dynamic scanning at corpus scale. OWASP ZAP
and Schemathesis are used only in Experiment~E4, against two applications, and never across
the corpus.

%==============================================================
\section{Design Justifications}
Three decisions visible in Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} will be challenged in review, and each has a
reason.

\textbf{1) The model sits after the scanner, not instead of it.} Rules stay authoritative for
what they cover, and the model adds ranking plus coverage of what rules miss. This is the same
arrangement Flynn \emph{et al.} used to prioritise alerts from multiple static analysis tools
with classification models~\cite{flynn}. The alternative --- replacing the linter with a model
--- would trade a deterministic, auditable check for a probabilistic one, which is the wrong
trade for a build gate.

\textbf{2) S2 scores every endpoint, not only the flagged ones.} Scanner output enters as
features that are zero when nothing fired, so an endpoint no rule touched can still score
high. Scoring only flagged endpoints would cap the model's recall at the scanner's recall and
make the generalisation research question unanswerable.

\textbf{3) Feedback never reaches the model directly.} Human verdicts enter through the label
store at the next deliberate retrain, for the reason given above.

\textbf{4) A tree ensemble rather than a language model.} A committed \texttt{model.json}
gives the same score for the same input on every run, while a hosted language model can change
between versions. Scoring 108{,}837 endpoints with a language model costs one call each, while
XGBoost scores a specification in one batched prediction. A SHAP attribution names the features
that moved a score, while a language model gives an explanation that sounds right but is not
tied to the score. Nevertheless a zero-shot language model is evaluated as baseline B4 in
Section~\ref{sec:method}, so the choice is tested and not assumed.

\textbf{Position against related work.} The related course topic ``Automatic API Security
Design Review for Microservices Using Threat Graph Analysis'' performs threat modelling once,
at design time, over a service graph. APIGUARD runs continuously inside the pipeline on every
commit, at endpoint granularity, and produces a numeric gate decision with a per-finding
explanation. The nearest published work, Cheh and Chen~\cite{cheh}, builds a graph model of
sensitive-field exposure in OpenAPI documents but evaluates it on a single API; recent surveys
of RESTful API vulnerability detection~\cite{tanveer} and of OpenAPI quality
analysis~\cite{oasquali} confirm that learned, explained risk scoring at corpus scale has not
been attempted. That is the gap this project addresses.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Research Questions Mapped to Experiments}
\label{tab:validation}
\centering
\scriptsize
\begin{tabularx}{\columnwidth}{L{2.05cm} L{1.75cm} Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Research question} & \textbf{Experiment} & \textbf{What is measured}\\
\midrule
\textbf{RQ1 Feasibility.} Does the model predict endpoint-level findings more accurately than
the scanner alone? & E1 --- provider-grouped 5-fold cross-validation, scored against reference labels (L2, L3) & PR-AUC, F1
and MCC against majority-class, scanner, logistic-regression, random-forest and language-model baselines.\\[2pt]
\textbf{RQ2 Generalisation.} With one OWASP category removed from training, does the model
still detect it? & E2 --- rule hold-out (the headline experiment) & Recall on the held-out
category's endpoints; above-chance recall means the model learned the design property, not the
rule trigger.\\[2pt]
\textbf{RQ3 False positives.} Does ranking reduce reading effort before the real issues? & E3
--- analyst effort on the L2 reference-labelled sample & Precision@$k$ and findings read to reach
all true positives, versus scanner severity ordering; $\tau$ swept.\\[2pt]
Supporting --- external validity & E4 --- VAmPI and crAPI & Whether known vulnerable endpoints
rank above benign ones, and VAmPI secure mode below vulnerable mode.\\[2pt]
Supporting --- runtime & E3 (same run) & Wall-clock time of S0--S3 on specifications of 10 to 500
endpoints.\\[2pt]
Supporting --- which features carry the signal & E5 --- ablation and SHAP & Performance with
each feature group removed, especially group~E; global SHAP importance.\\
