%==============================================================
\section{Deployment View}
APIGUARD ships as a command-line tool that reads a file and returns an exit code, so it fits
any CI system without a dedicated integration. A GitHub Actions job is the reference
deployment:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
- name: API security gate
  run: |
    apiguard scan api/openapi.yaml \
      --model models/model_l2plus.json \
      --threshold 0.70 \
      --max-above 0 \
      --format sarif --out apiguard.sarif
- uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3
  with:
    sarif_file: apiguard.sarif
\end{lstlisting}

The model artefact is committed to the repository alongside the pipeline definition, so a
build is reproducible from the checkout alone. Target runtime is a few seconds for a
specification of a few hundred endpoints: S1 dominates, since S2b is a single batched
prediction over a feature table. The gate can be run in report-only mode by setting a large
$k$, which is the recommended way to introduce it to an existing API before it is allowed to
block a build.

%==============================================================
\section{How the System Model Will Be Validated}
The system model above defines what the experiments must measure.
Table~\ref{tab:validation} maps each research question to the experiment that answers it.

E2 is the experiment that answers the obvious criticism of the whole design --- that a model
trained on scanner labels has only learned the scanner. It is protected in the schedule ahead of
every optional component; the cut order is given in Section~\ref{sec:method}.


%==============================================================
\section{Proposed Methodology and Algorithm}
\label{sec:method}
This section turns the system model into a procedure that can be run and checked. It fixes
what a label means, how labels are produced and kept honest, how features are computed, how
the model is trained and tuned, how the gate judges a change, and how each experiment is
carried out. Fig.~\ref{fig:method} shows the order of the steps. One rule runs through all of
them: \emph{no result is ever measured against the scanner labels the model was trained on.}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{methodology.pdf}
\caption{Methodology in eight steps. The top row builds the dataset; the bottom row, read
right to left, labels it, trains the model and evaluates it. Amber marks shared code and the
model artefact; the dashed teal box marks the reference labels (L2, L3).}
\label{fig:method}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Problem Statement and Label Definition}
\label{sec:m-prob}
Given an endpoint $e$ and its feature vector $x(e)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$, learn a function $f$
whose output $r(e)=f(x(e))$ ranks endpoints by the chance that they carry a security-relevant
\emph{design} issue. The label $y(e)=1$ when the specification gives evidence of at least one
issue in an OWASP API Top 10 category that a specification can show~\cite{owasptop10}:
API1 (object-level authorisation), API2 (authentication), API3 (property-level exposure),
API4 (resource consumption), API5 (function-level authorisation), API8 (misconfiguration)
and API9 (improper inventory). API6, API7 and API10 rarely leave evidence in a specification;
they are recorded as ``not assessable'' and are never counted as negatives.

\subsection{Corpus Preparation}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Snapshot.} Download the APIs.guru directory once and store the snapshot date,
the source URL of every file and the directory commit. All counts in the final report are
counts of this snapshot.
\item \textbf{Parse.} Run S0 on every file. Files that fail are logged with a reason and the
parse-failure rate is reported.
\item \textbf{De-duplicate.} Drop files whose text hash is already present. Exact hashes miss
near-copies, so the next step also protects against them.
\item \textbf{Group.} Give every endpoint a group identifier equal to its provider (the
directory name in APIs.guru, the repository in APIstic). Every split in this section is made
on groups, never on rows. Leakage between training and test data is a known cause of
over-optimistic results in machine-learning studies~\cite{kapoor}.
\item \textbf{Specification version.} The OWASP ruleset targets OpenAPI 3.x, so Swagger~2.0
files receive fewer scanner labels for a reason unrelated to security. Scanner (L1) training
labels are therefore taken from OpenAPI~3.x files only. Swagger~2.0 endpoints are still
scored, and their results are reported separately. The feature \texttt{is\_oas3} is constant
on the training rows; it is kept so that the feature schema stays stable.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Labelling Protocol (T2)}
Three label sources are used, and each has a fixed job (Table~\ref{tab:labels}).

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{What Each Label Source May Be Used For}
\label{tab:labels}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\columnwidth}{L{1.5cm} L{1.5cm} L{1.2cm} Y}
\toprule
\textbf{Label} & \textbf{Made by} & \textbf{Train} & \textbf{Evaluate}\\
\midrule
L1 & Scanner & Yes & Never\\
L2 & Three LLM passes & Train folds only & Test folds\\
L3 & Documented flaws & No & Yes\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\textbf{L1, weak labels.} $y_1(e)=1$ when the scanner reports at least one finding of
severity \texttt{error} or \texttt{warn} on $e$, ignoring rules that fire on more than 15\% of all
endpoints (a pilot scan showed that, without this, over 85\% of endpoints are flagged and the label carries no signal;
the ignored rules are listed in the dataset statistics file). These labels are cheap and noisy. They are
weak supervision in the sense of Ratner \emph{et al.}~\cite{snorkel}, and they are used for
training only.

\textbf{L2, reference labels.} A sample of 304 endpoints (plus a pilot of 32 that is
discarded) is drawn from the de-duplicated corpus at random within strata: flagged or not
flagged by the scanner, read method or write method, and small or large parent specification.
Because the sample is stratified, every reported metric is weighted by the inverse of the
sampling probability, so that it estimates the value on the whole corpus. \emph{The L2 labels
were produced by language models, not by people.} The procedure is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Write a codebook with one decision rule per category of Section~\ref{sec:m-prob}.
\item Run a pilot of 32 endpoints, revise the codebook, and discard the pilot from the 304.
\item Three independent labelling passes (two Claude passes and one Codex pass) label all 304
endpoints without seeing scanner output, model scores or the sensitive-term lexicon. The
sheets given to the labellers contain only the endpoint, its specification excerpt and the codebook.
\item Report agreement before combining: pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$ was 0.49 to 0.58 and
Fleiss' $\kappa$ was 0.54, below the target of 0.6 that was set in advance. The codebook was
revised once after a pass misread an optional query-token rule; this is the only revision.
\item The final label is the majority vote of the three passes (62 of 304 endpoints, 20\%
positive); the category is the union of the categories of the passes that voted 1.
\end{enumerate}
Agreement between labellers does not show that they are right, and agreement between language
models is not independent evidence, so L3 is the external anchor. The agreement below target,
and the fact that no person checked the labels, are reported as threats to validity.

\textbf{L3, ground truth.} Endpoints of VAmPI and crAPI whose flaws are documented by their
authors (for crAPI, the 15 challenges listed by the OWASP project~\cite{crapichallenges}). The documented flaws are mapped to endpoints, and to OWASP API Top 10 2023 categories, by a language model that reads the project README and the handler source; no person reviewed this mapping. Many of these flaws live in the implementation and cannot be seen in a specification,
so L3 is reported twice: for all documented flaws, and for the subset visible at design level
(missing authentication, object-level access, excessive data in responses).

\subsection{Feature Extraction (S2a)}
\label{sec:m-feat}
S2a computes $d=66$ features in six groups (Table~\ref{tab:features}): A structural~(18),
B security scheme~(14), C data sensitivity~(7), D documentation~(11), E scanner-derived~(14)
and F context~(2). The implementation is a pure function with a fixed output order, no random
numbers, and no null values. Two choices need a justification.

\emph{Group C uses a lexicon.} It maps tokens of property names to weights 1 to 3: 3 for
credentials, payment and government identifiers, 2 for tokens, sessions and sensitive personal
attributes, 1 for ordinary personal data. Names are split on case changes and punctuation, so
\texttt{dateOfBirth} and \texttt{date\_of\_birth} match the same term. The list is compiled
from the OWASP API3:2023 examples and common personal-data categories, frozen before any
labelling, and is English only. Property names in other languages are not matched; this is a
stated limitation.

\emph{The provider is never a feature.} It is used only to group splits, so the model cannot
learn who wrote a specification.

\subsection{Training Algorithm (T4)}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:train} gives the procedure. The unit of every split is a provider. The
loss is the logistic loss of XGBoost~\cite{xgboost}. The positive class is a small minority,
so the positive-class weight is set from the training fold,
\begin{equation}
\texttt{scale\_pos\_weight}=\frac{N_{\text{neg}}}{N_{\text{pos}}},
\end{equation}
and models are compared with PR-AUC~\cite{saito}, F1 and the Matthews correlation
coefficient~\cite{chicco}, never accuracy.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Grouped training and tuning (T4)}
\label{alg:train}
\footnotesize
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE endpoints with features $x$, weak label $y_1$, reference label $y_2$ (where present), group $g$
\ENSURE model $f$, predictions on every test fold
\STATE split the groups into 5 folds $P_1,\dots,P_5$ with no group in two folds
\FOR{$i=1$ \TO $5$}
  \STATE $T\leftarrow$ endpoints with $g\notin P_i$;\; test set $\leftarrow$ endpoints with $g\in P_i$
  \STATE label of each $e\in T$ is $y_2(e)$ if it exists, else $y_1(e)$
  \STATE draw 30 hyperparameter settings at random (depth 3--8, learning rate 0.03--0.3, subsample, column sample, min.\ child weight, $\lambda$)
  \FOR{each setting}
    \STATE inner 3-fold cross-validation on $T$, again grouped by provider; train with early stopping on the inner validation part
    \STATE record mean PR-AUC
  \ENDFOR
  \STATE refit the best setting on all of $T$ with \texttt{scale\_pos\_weight} from $T$
  \STATE score the test set; for L2 and L3 endpoints keep the score and the reference label
\ENDFOR
\STATE repeat with 5 random seeds; report mean and interval over the seeds and folds
\STATE fit the final model on all groups and write \texttt{model.json} with seed, tool versions, schema versions and corpus date
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Four baselines are trained on the same feature table and scored on the same folds:
B0 the majority class; B1 the scanner itself (flagged is 1, optionally ordered by severity);
B2 logistic regression on standardised features; B3 random forest. A fifth baseline, B4, asks
a large language model for a risk score of the normalised endpoint in a fixed prompt at
temperature~0, with the model name, version and date recorded. B4 is run only on the L2 sample,
because calling it on 100{,}000 endpoints is not affordable, and its cost and latency are
reported next to its accuracy.

\subsection{Threshold and Gate Algorithm (S3)}
The threshold $\tau$ is chosen on validation folds, never on test folds. Three operating
points are reported: the $\tau$ that maximises F1 (the default), the smallest $\tau$ with
precision at least 0.9, and the largest $\tau$ with recall at least 0.9. No post-hoc
calibration is applied, so that the score stays the logistic transform of the margin that SHAP
explains; a calibration curve is reported as a diagnostic.

As specified, the gate of~\eqref{eq:gate} rescans every endpoint, so a legacy API with old
problems would fail every build. The gate therefore counts only endpoints that are new or
changed relative to the base branch. Let $S_b$ and $S_h$ be the normalised base and head
specifications, let $\mathrm{key}(e)=(m,p)$ be the method and path of $e$, and let $h(e)$ be the hash
of the normalised record without file-identity fields. Then
\begin{multline}
\Delta(S_h,S_b)=\bigl\{\,e\in E(S_h)\;:\;\nexists\,e'\in E(S_b),\\
\mathrm{key}(e')=\mathrm{key}(e)\wedge h(e')=h(e)\,\bigr\},
\end{multline}
\begin{equation}
G_{\Delta}(S_h,S_b)=
\begin{cases}
1, & \bigl|\{\,e\in\Delta(S_h,S_b)\;:\;r(e)\ge\tau\,\}\bigr|>k,\\
0, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\label{eq:gatedelta}
\end{equation}
The report still lists every endpoint in risk order and marks which ones were in scope. If no
base exists, every endpoint is new. A renamed path counts as a new endpoint. The endpoint
identifier of S0 cannot be used for matching, because it contains the specification hash and
changes whenever any line of the file changes. Matching costs $O(n)$ in the number of
endpoints.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Runtime scan with changed-endpoint gate (S0--S3)}
\label{alg:scan}
\footnotesize
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE head specification $S_h$, optional base specification $S_b$, model $f$, threshold $\tau$, tolerance $k$
\ENSURE ranked report and exit code
\STATE $E_h\leftarrow$ S0$(S_h)$;\; $E_b\leftarrow$ S0$(S_b)$ if $S_b$ is given, else $\emptyset$
\STATE $A\leftarrow$ S1$(S_h)$ \COMMENT{findings attributed to endpoints}
\FORALL{$e\in E_h$}
  \STATE $x(e)\leftarrow$ S2a$(e,A(e),|E_h|)$;\; $r(e)\leftarrow f(x(e))$
\ENDFOR
\STATE $\Delta\leftarrow$ endpoints of $E_h$ that are new or changed relative to $E_b$ (all if $S_b$ is absent)
\STATE $n\leftarrow|\{e\in\Delta : r(e)\ge\tau\}|$
\STATE compute SHAP for the 10 highest-scoring endpoints; write the report ranked by $r(e)$, marking $\Delta$
\RETURN $1$ if $n>k$, else $0$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Experiment Protocols}
All results use the same grouped folds, five seeds, and a provider-level bootstrap with 1{,}000
resamples for 95\% intervals. Two methods are compared on the same resamples.

\textbf{E1, accuracy against reference labels.} Algorithm~\ref{alg:train} is run and every model
is scored on the L2 and L3 endpoints of its test folds. The scanner is scored against the
same reference labels, so the comparison with the model is fair. Per-version results (OpenAPI~3.x
and Swagger~2.0) are reported separately.

\textbf{E2, rule hold-out.} This answers RQ2. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the categories with at
least 20 positive endpoints in L2 and L3. For each $c\in\mathcal{C}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Remove from the training set every endpoint whose only findings are in category $c$.
\item Recompute every group-E feature from the findings with all category-$c$ findings
deleted, so that no feature carries any trace of $c$.
\item Train as in Algorithm~\ref{alg:train}.
\item On test folds, take the L2 and L3 endpoints whose reference label is category $c$ as
positives, and the endpoints labelled 0 as negatives. Report PR-AUC and recall at $\tau$.
\end{enumerate}
The reference for chance is the prevalence of $c$, and the reference for the best case is the
model trained with $c$ included. A category counts as \emph{generalised} when the lower end of
the 95\% interval of (PR-AUC minus prevalence) is above zero. The answer to RQ2 is the number
of categories that generalise out of $|\mathcal{C}|$, reported with its intervals. This
criterion is fixed here, before any result exists.

\textbf{E3, analyst effort and speed.} On the L2 sample, report precision at $k=5,10,20$ and the
number of findings read before all true positives are found, for the model ranking against the
scanner severity ranking, with $\tau$ swept over 0.1 to 0.9. Wall-clock time of S0 to S3 is
measured per component, 10 runs per specification, on specifications of 10 to 500 endpoints,
which tests the requirement that the runtime lane finishes in seconds.

\textbf{E4, external validity.} VAmPI and crAPI are scored by the final model, which never saw
them. Report the area under the ROC curve and the rank of each documented-flaw endpoint among
all endpoints. OWASP ZAP and Schemathesis are run against the live applications to give an
independent dynamic finding set; the overlap with the highest-ranked endpoints is reported.
These tools are used here only, never on the corpus.

\textbf{E5, ablation and explanation.} Remove each feature group in turn, and also remove
group E alone, and report the loss in PR-AUC. Report mean absolute SHAP values globally.
SHAP values for tree ensembles are computed exactly with TreeSHAP~\cite{treeshap}. They explain the raw
margin (log-odds), so the attributions sum to the margin minus the base margin, not to $r(e)$
itself. A \emph{text-inflation test} then checks whether the model can be gamed: for L2
negatives, set the group-D length features to the 90th percentile of the training data, change
nothing else, and report the mean score change and the share of endpoints that cross $\tau$.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\textbf{Data.} The APIs.guru directory holds 2{,}529 APIs with 108{,}837 endpoints before
de-duplication~\cite{apisguru}; counts after de-duplication and parsing are reported from the
frozen snapshot. VAmPI~\cite{vampi} and crAPI~\cite{crapi} are used for L3 and E4 only.

\textbf{Software.} Python, XGBoost, Spectral and the ruleset are pinned in a requirements file.
The feature schema (\texttt{s2a-1.0}) and the lexicon (\texttt{lex-1.0}) carry version labels
that are written into \texttt{model.json}, so a result can be traced to the exact code that
produced it. Every random seed is fixed and recorded.

\textbf{Hardware.} Training and all experiments run on one CPU workstation; the tabular
feature table does not need a GPU. Runtime in E3 is measured on a GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runner,
which is the reference deployment, and on the team's laptop, 10 runs per specification, with
the median and the range reported. Network time is excluded.

\textbf{Metrics.} PR-AUC, F1 and MCC for classification; precision at $k$ and findings read
for analyst effort; milliseconds per specification for speed; mean absolute SHAP value for
explanation. All intervals are 95\% provider-level bootstrap intervals.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} The code, tests, feature schema and the figure scripts are kept in
one repository, and the model file used for each reported table is committed with it: \texttt{model.json} for E1 to E4, and \texttt{model\_l2plus.json} (trained on the 608 reference labels of E7) for the packaged tool.

\subsection{Threats to Validity}
\textbf{Label noise.} L1 contains scanner errors; this is why it is never used for evaluation.
\textbf{Small, model-labelled sample.} 304 endpoints give wide intervals, and the labels come from three language-model passes with Fleiss' $\kappa=0.54$ and no human check; intervals are reported rather
than hidden, and L3, whose flaws are documented by the VAmPI authors, is the external anchor (the flaw-to-endpoint mapping was done by a language model reading the handler source). \textbf{Specification versus implementation.} Design-level scores cannot see
implementation flaws; E4 measures the gap. \textbf{Corpus bias.} APIs.guru is dominated by
public commercial APIs and may not represent internal APIs. \textbf{Language.} The lexicon is
English only. \textbf{Tool drift.} A new Spectral or ruleset version changes L1 and the
baseline together, so versions are pinned. \textbf{Language-model baseline.} Output can change
between model versions; the exact model and date are recorded, and B4 is a point of comparison,
not part of the system.

\subsection{Cut Order if the Schedule Slips}
Items are cut in this order: the APIstic scale-up, the 42Crunch audit score, and B4 on any
sample larger than L2. E1 to E5, and in particular E2, are never cut.

%==============================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
All numbers come from the frozen run of 6 November 2026 on 80{,}974 endpoints from 665 providers,
evaluated on the 304 L2 endpoints (62 positive, 20\%). Intervals are 95\% provider-level bootstrap intervals.

\subsection{E1: Accuracy}
Table~\ref{tab:e1} gives the mean over five seeds. The model is twice as good as always guessing
the positive class (PR-AUC 0.21) and clearly better than logistic regression (difference 0.014 to 0.189).
It does \emph{not} beat the scanner alone: the difference to the scanner is $-0.20$ to $+0.16$,
which includes zero.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{E1: Accuracy on L2 (mean of five seeds)}
\label{tab:e1}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{PR-AUC} & \textbf{ROC-AUC}\\
\midrule
Always positive & 0.21 & 0.50\\
Scanner only (B1) & 0.47 & 0.69\\
Logistic regression & 0.33 & 0.72\\
XGBoost (APIGUARD) & 0.42 & 0.70\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{E2: Rule Hold-out (RQ2)}
Only 3 of the 10 categories had the 20 positives needed for a test. The model generalised on one of
them (Table~\ref{tab:e2}). The answer to RQ2 is therefore \textbf{1 of 3 tested categories}, and the other
seven could not be tested with this sample.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{E2: Held-out category, PR-AUC against prevalence}
\label{tab:e2}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Cat.} & \textbf{Pos.} & \textbf{Prev.} & \textbf{PR-AUC} & \textbf{ROC-AUC} & \textbf{Generalised}\\
\midrule
API2 & 54 & 0.18 & 0.22 & 0.57 & No\\
API3 & 24 & 0.10 & 0.15 & 0.50 & No\\
API5 & 34 & 0.09 & 0.37 & 0.91 & Yes\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{E3: Effort and Speed}
Ranking by model score did not reduce reviewer effort compared with scanner severity: precision at 5,
10 and 20 was 0.20, 0.30 and 0.20 for the model against 0.60, 0.40 and 0.55 for the scanner.
The precision and effort numbers use the out-of-fold scores of E1, so no test endpoint was seen in training. The best F1 over the threshold sweep was 0.47. Run time from S0 to S3 was 1.5 s for the 14-endpoint VAmPI specification and 10.5 s for the largest (422 endpoints). The Spectral scan (S1) takes 1.4 s and 5.6 s, normalisation (S0) 0.07 s and 2.7 s, and the feature builder (S2a) 0.004 s and 2.1 s; the model (S2b) and the SHAP explanation (S2c) together take under 25 ms in every run.

\subsection{E4: External Check on VAmPI and crAPI}
VAmPI has 14 endpoints, 10 with a documented flaw and 5 visible at design level. The ROC-AUC over all
flaws was 0.48 (PR-AUC 0.77 against a prevalence of 0.71) and over the design-visible flaws 0.22; the
design-visible endpoints ranked 8th to 12th of 14. crAPI has 44 endpoints, 15 mapped to the 15 challenges that
the OWASP project documents~\cite{crapichallenges}, and 3 of them visible at design level. The ROC-AUC over
all flaws was 0.39 (PR-AUC 0.30 against a prevalence of 0.34) and over the design-visible flaws 0.16; those
three endpoints ranked 31st, 38th and 40th of 44.

This is a negative result on both applications: the model does not rank flawed endpoints above others, and it
is worse than a random ranking on the flaws that a specification can show. The ranked tables show why. The
highest scores go to authentication and write endpoints that the scanner flags many times, and the lowest scores
go to read (GET) endpoints that declare no authentication, which are the clearest design-level flaw
(\texttt{/users/v1/\_debug} in VAmPI, \texttt{receive\_report} in crAPI). A model trained to agree with scanner
and language-model labels inherits whatever they under-weight. Two cautions apply: each application is small, so
no interval is given, and the flaw-to-endpoint mapping was made by a language model from the project documentation and handler
source (Section~\ref{sec:m-prob}). The dynamic comparison with OWASP ZAP and Schemathesis planned for E4 was not run in this study and
remains future work. Section~\ref{sec:e6} tests a likely cause.

\subsection{E5: Ablation and Explanation}
Removing the structural group A cost the most (PR-AUC 0.44 to 0.30). The other groups changed it by at most 0.04.
Mean absolute SHAP was highest for the security-scheme group B, then scanner-derived E and structural A.
The text-inflation test did not game the model: setting description lengths to the 90th percentile moved
the mean score by $-0.011$ and only 0.7\% of negatives crossed $\tau$.

\subsection{E6: Training Only on the Reference Labels}
\label{sec:e6}
The E4 failure has a likely cause in how E1 to E4 were trained. About 81{,}000 of the training labels are scanner (L1) labels,
and L1 ignores every rule that fires on more than 15\% of endpoints. That list includes \texttt{owasp:api2:2023-read-restricted},
the rule for a read endpoint without authentication. So the model was taught that such endpoints are normal. The reference labels
say otherwise: among the 304 L2 endpoints, 47\% of those with no security requirement are positive against 5\% of those with one.
E6 therefore trains the model \emph{only} on the 304 reference labels, with the same provider-grouped five-fold protocol and five seeds,
and compares it with the scanner on the same test folds. Only this one variant was run. It was motivated by the E4 result and by the
label rates above, so E4 is no longer a clean external test for it; the E6 test folds are, because they are out-of-fold.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{E6: Trained on L2 only, out-of-fold on L2 (mean of five seeds)}
\label{tab:e6}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{PR-AUC} & \textbf{ROC-AUC} & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{MCC}\\
\midrule
Scanner only (B1) & 0.47 & 0.69 & 0.43 & 0.26\\
Logistic regression & 0.47 & 0.81 & 0.52 & 0.38\\
XGBoost, L2 only & 0.48 & 0.80 & 0.52 & 0.39\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

On the seed-averaged out-of-fold scores the ROC-AUC of XGBoost is 0.84 against 0.69 for the scanner, and the
provider-level bootstrap interval of the difference is $+0.01$ to $+0.33$, which excludes zero only narrowly.
The PR-AUC difference is $-0.20$ to $+0.37$ and includes zero. On the two external applications the
L2-only model gave ROC-AUC 0.86 on VAmPI against 0.48 before (design-visible flaws 0.72 against 0.22), and 0.39 on crAPI
against 0.39 before (design-visible flaws 0.63 against 0.16). So it fixed VAmPI and not crAPI. The effort measures of E3 are repeated with the out-of-fold scores of this model in Section~\ref{sec:e8}. Because the labels are language-model labels, E6 measures agreement
with those labels; the two applications are the only evidence that does not depend on them.

\subsection{E7: Doubling the Reference Labels}
\label{sec:e7}
To test whether label count is the limit, 304 further endpoints were labelled by one language-model pass with the same
codebook and the same stratified draw, excluding every endpoint of the test sample and the pilot (67 positives, 22\%). They are used for
training only; the test set is still the original 304 endpoints, scored out-of-fold, and the extra endpoints of a provider that is in a
test fold are not used for that fold. Because it is a single pass, no agreement score exists for these labels.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{E7: XGBoost on the original test set, mean of five seeds}
\label{tab:e7}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Training labels} & \textbf{PR-AUC} & \textbf{ROC-AUC} & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{MCC}\\
\midrule
Scanner only (B1) & 0.47 & 0.69 & 0.43 & 0.26\\
L2 only (E6, 304) & 0.48 & 0.80 & 0.52 & 0.39\\
L2 + extra (608) & 0.52 & 0.81 & 0.51 & 0.40\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Doubling the labels did not change the result measurably: the difference to E6 is $-0.16$ to $+0.22$ in PR-AUC and $-0.05$ to $+0.05$
in ROC-AUC. Against the scanner, the seed-averaged ROC-AUC is 0.84 against 0.69, with a difference of $+0.03$ to $+0.32$, and the PR-AUC
difference is $-0.11$ to $+0.34$. On the external applications the model trained on 608 labels reached ROC-AUC 0.79 on VAmPI and
0.37 on crAPI. So more labels of this kind did not fix crAPI, and label count alone is not the limit; label quality and coverage of the
flaws that crAPI contains (mostly implementation flaws) are more likely limits.

\subsection{The Packaged Tool}
The command line was packaged as a single Windows executable with PyInstaller (about 410 MB, because it carries XGBoost and SHAP).
It needs Node.js and the Spectral package next to it for the scanner step. Run on VAmPI (14 endpoints) and crAPI (44 endpoints)
with \texttt{model\_l2plus.json} and the model's own threshold $\tau=0.53$, it printed the ranked endpoints with their top SHAP reasons
and the scanner rules that fired, and ended with ``GATE: FAIL'' and exit code 1 on both, the expected verdict for applications built to
be vulnerable. The gate was not tested on a clean specification, so its false-alarm rate on that kind of input is known only from the E1 and E3
measurements above.

\subsection{E8: Scanner and Model Combined}
\label{sec:e8}
E3 asked whether the model ranks findings better than the scanner and the answer was no. The design places the model \emph{after} the scanner, so
E8 tests the combination: the score is the mean of the percentile rank given by the scanner (severity, then rule count) and the percentile rank given by
the model. This one setting was fixed in advance and no weight was tried. All scores are out-of-fold on the original 304 test endpoints, averaged over the five seeds;
precision at $k$ and the findings read before the last true positive follow the definitions of E3.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{E8: Ranking on the test set (model trained on the 608 reference labels of E7)}
\label{tab:e8}
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Ranking} & \textbf{PR-AUC} & \textbf{ROC} & \textbf{P@5} & \textbf{P@10} & \textbf{P@20} & \textbf{Read}\\
\midrule
Scanner only & 0.47 & 0.69 & 0.60 & 0.40 & 0.55 & 272\\
Model only & 0.52 & 0.81 & 0.64 & 0.60 & 0.57 & 279\\
Scanner + model & 0.55 & 0.80 & 0.88 & 0.76 & 0.66 & 245\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Against the scanner the combination gains $+0.03$ to $+0.30$ in PR-AUC and $+0.05$ to $+0.24$ in ROC-AUC (provider-level 95\% intervals, both excluding zero), and a reviewer
who reads the top 10 finds 7.6 true positives on average in expectation against 4.0 (precision 0.76 against 0.40). Against the model alone the differences are not
significant ($-0.13$ to $+0.19$ in PR-AUC). The model trained only on 304 labels (E6) gives a smaller gain whose PR-AUC interval includes zero ($-0.16$ to $+0.27$). Two caution points: this is the best of the combinations
reported here only in the sense that it is the one we specified, but the two training policies and the two metrics make four comparisons, so the intervals should be read as indicative; and precision at $k$ has no interval because it is computed on a single sample of 304 endpoints with 62 positives.

\subsection{Discussion and Limitations}
The honest conclusion has two parts. Trained on scanner labels (E1 to E4), APIGUARD matches the scanner at similar accuracy and the same cost, generalises to one
unseen category, and does not beat the scanner. Trained only on the reference labels (E6), it beats the scanner on ROC-AUC (difference $+0.01$ to $+0.33$) and on F1 and MCC, but not
significantly on PR-AUC, and it still ranks crAPI flaws no better than chance, even with twice as many reference labels (E7). Combined with the scanner (E8), it ranks better than the scanner alone in both PR-AUC and ROC-AUC, which is the use the design intends. Four things limit this. (i)~The reference labels were produced by
language models, with Fleiss' $\kappa=0.54$ and no human check, so noise in them caps every score. (ii)~Only 62 positives exist,
so most categories were untestable. (iii)~L1 labels are the scanner's own, so a model trained on them can at best
approximate it. (iv)~On the two external applications the scanner-trained model ranked flawed endpoints no better than chance (E4), and the L2-trained model did well on VAmPI but not on crAPI (E6), so the claim is limited to the corpus labels, and the dynamic ZAP/Schemathesis comparison was not run. A larger, human-labelled sample and more vulnerable-by-design applications would be the first things to add.

\textbf{Sensitivity to the labels.} The analysis was repeated with an earlier key made from two passes
plus an adjudication (46 positives). E1 was similar (XGBoost 0.46, scanner 0.43, difference $-0.21$ to $+0.30$).
E2 changed: with that key only API2 and API5 had enough positives, and both generalised (2 of 2).
So the E2 answer depends on the label key. API5 generalises under both keys; API2 does not under the majority key.
We report the majority-vote result as the main one, because it was fixed before the sensitivity run, and
treat RQ2 as unresolved at this sample size.

%==============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
This deliverable has specified the APIGUARD system model: three lanes separated by when they
run and how far they are trusted, a runtime path of four components from specification to CI
exit code, an offline path that produces the model artefact those components load, and a
feedback path that returns human verdicts to the label store without ever closing a loop into
the model. The notation and the gate rule in~\eqref{eq:scored} and~\eqref{eq:gate} fix what
the implementation must compute, and the interface contracts fix what each component must
guarantee. Section~\ref{sec:method} turns this model into the proposed methodology and algorithm: the label
protocol that keeps scanner labels out of every evaluation, the feature extraction, the grouped
training procedure, a gate that judges only new or changed endpoints, and the protocol for the
rule hold-out experiment that the whole design rests on. The experiments of Section~\ref{sec:results} show that the model matches but does not beat the scanner, and that its
rule hold-out generalisation is limited to one of three testable categories.

